While you decide, here are useful clarifying questions and a concrete agenda to help you prepare for your annual physical and decide about endocrinology referral.

Clarifying questions for you (so I can tailor recommendations):
- Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or are you asking about screening/risk management?
- What units is your last creatinine in (mg/dL or µmol/L)? (A creatinine of 320 µmol/L ≈ 3.6 mg/dL — that’s important for kidney function assessment.)
- Do you know your most recent eGFR, HbA1c, fasting glucose, urine albumin/creatinine, or lipid panel?
- Are you currently on any glucose-lowering medications (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 agonists, insulin, sulfonylureas)?
- How is your blood pressure and weight control? Any history of hypoglycemia?

Suggested questions to bring to Dr. James Wilson (PCP) about diabetes risk/management
- Do I currently meet criteria for diabetes (please review my latest HbA1c or fasting glucose)? If not, how often should I be screened?
- Given my age (55), atrial fibrillation on apixaban, metoprolol, prednisone taper, and elevated creatinine, what HbA1c target do you recommend for me?
- How will my kidney function (current creatinine 320) affect choice and dosing of diabetes medications? Are any agents contraindicated or require dose adjustment?
- With the prednisone taper, should I expect higher glucose readings and do you recommend increased home monitoring while on steroids? If so, what targets and frequency?
- If lifestyle changes aren’t enough, which medication classes are safest and most effective for me (considering CKD, AF, CV risk, and depression history)?
- Are SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists appropriate for me given my kidney function and cardiovascular profile?
- Do any of my current meds (apixaban, metoprolol, antidepressants) interact with diabetes drugs or affect monitoring?
- Do you recommend checking these labs now: HbA1c, fasting glucose, comprehensive metabolic panel (for creatinine/eGFR), urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, lipid panel, and liver tests?
- Should I have an eye exam, foot exam, neuropathy screen, and vaccinations (e.g., influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19) as part of diabetes prevention/management?
- At what point would you refer me to an endocrinologist (e.g., A1c threshold, medication complexity, declining kidney function)?

When to consider referring to an endocrinologist (reasons to request Phoenix Endocrinology)
- New diagnosis with very high HbA1c or severe hyperglycemia requiring insulin initiation.
- Poor glycemic control despite multiple glucose-lowering drugs / complex regimen needed.
- Recurrent or severe hypoglycemia or very labile blood sugars.
- Significant chronic kidney disease (if creatinine/eGFR indicates advanced CKD) and you need guidance on safe, kidney-appropriate therapies.
- Need for expert management of cardiometabolic therapies (GLP-1 or SGLT2) in the context of complex comorbidities.
- Diagnostic uncertainty (e.g., atypical diabetes, secondary causes).
If you meet any of the above, a referral is reasonable — otherwise your PCP may manage initially and refer if needed.

Practical checklist to bring to the appointment
- Recent labs (or request them beforehand): HbA1c, BMP (creatinine/eGFR), urine albumin/creatinine, lipid panel, liver enzymes.
- Current medication list (doses) and allergies.
- Recent home blood glucose log (if available) or any symptomatic episodes.
- Weight, blood pressure records, and list of specialists (cardiology, nephrology).
- Explicit ask: “If you think endocrinology is appropriate, can you place the referral to Phoenix Endocrinology now?”

Notes about meds and kidney function (brief)
- Many diabetes drug choices depend on eGFR. Metformin and some others have eGFR cutoffs or dose adjustments; SGLT2s have eGFR considerations but may be beneficial for kidney/CV protection down to certain eGFR values — this depends on exact eGFR and drug.
- Prednisone (steroids) commonly increases blood glucose while on the taper; monitoring and temporary therapy adjustments may be needed.
- Apixaban is an anticoagulant — it doesn’t directly contraindicate most diabetes meds, but any invasive procedures or changes to other meds require coordination.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft an email to Dr. Wilson summarizing these questions and requesting labs or a referral (I can draft only, or send it if you want me to send).
- Draft a short message requesting a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology for review of diabetes management (you can review before sending).
- Forward this conversation to your health proxy (vince2000@gmail.com) — I will only do that if you explicitly tell me to.

