I can help — this is a good, organized set of things to bring/ask at your annual physical and to use to decide whether you need an endocrinology referral. Before I give tailored recommendations, two quick clarifying questions:

1) Do you already have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes? If yes, what are your most recent A1c and home glucose readings (fasting and post‑prandial)?  
2) When you say “last creatinine was 320” can you confirm the units and the date of that test (320 µmol/L vs 3.20 mg/dL vs something else)? That matters a lot for medication choices because it changes your eGFR estimate.

While you check those, here are practical, clinician‑focused points and a compact checklist you can use at the visit.

Important context & why it matters
- Prednisone (even a taper) can raise blood glucose substantially — you may need extra monitoring and temporary medication changes while on steroids.  
- Renal impairment (high creatinine / low eGFR) strongly affects which diabetes drugs are safe (e.g., metformin and many SGLT2 inhibitor thresholds) and increases hypoglycemia risk with insulin or sulfonylureas.  
- You’re on apixaban and metoprolol for atrial fibrillation — anticoagulation increases stakes for any hypoglycemia-related falls/episodes and some drug interactions/dose decisions consider renal function. Coordinate any med changes with your PCP and cardiology as needed.  
- History of depression matters because some diabetes meds (GLP‑1s can cause nausea/weight loss) and hypoglycemia events can worsen mood and adherence — good to mention.

Labs and data to get (bring copies or have done before the visit)
- A1c (most recent)  
- Fasting glucose and any home glucose logs (fasting, pre/post meal) — bring 1–2 weeks if available  
- Basic metabolic panel: serum creatinine and eGFR, electrolytes (very important) — confirm date/units of that “320” result  
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) — to screen for diabetic kidney disease  
- Lipid panel  
- LFTs and TSH (if not recent)  
- Weight, BMI, blood pressure readings (home readings if available)  
- Medication list with doses (including prednisone dose/timing, apixaban, metoprolol, antidepressants) and any recent med changes

Questions to ask Dr. James Wilson (PCP) at the appointment
- Do I currently meet criteria for diabetes (ask to review my A1c and glucose trends)?  
- Given my creatinine of “320” (confirm units/date), what is my eGFR and how does that limit diabetes medication options?  
- While I’m on prednisone, how should I monitor blood glucose and when should I be started on or have adjustments to diabetes meds? (If you have home glucose meter, ask what thresholds require temporary insulin.)  
- Are any diabetes medicines unsafe now because of my kidney function (e.g., metformin, dose adjustments or stop)?  
- Would you consider starting or switching to an agent that reduces cardiovascular/renal risk (GLP‑1 RA or SGLT2 inhibitor)? If so, is my kidney function appropriate for them?  
- Do you recommend a referral to an endocrinologist? If not now, under what circumstances would you refer?  
- Do any planned medication changes affect my apixaban dose or bleeding risk? Should cardiology be involved if diabetes meds change?  
- Any screening I should have today for diabetes complications (retinopathy, neuropathy, foot exam, vaccinations)?

Red flags / reasons to refer to endocrinology now
- A1c persistently above goal despite reasonable therapy (for example A1c ≥9% or progressive hyperglycemia).  
- Need to start or intensify insulin (especially if you or PCP are not comfortable managing basal/bolus insulin and dose titration).  
- Complex comorbidities that complicate management: advanced CKD (eGFR low enough to restrict many drug choices), steroid‑induced hyperglycemia that’s difficult to control, recurrent severe hypoglycemia, or rapid changes in glucose control.  
- Need for specialized agents for cardiorenal benefit and complex renal dosing decisions.  
- Diagnostic uncertainty (e.g., atypical presentation, possible secondary causes).

Medication considerations (high level)
- Metformin: commonly avoided or dose‑reduced at eGFR <45 mL/min/1.73m2 and generally contraindicated at eGFR <30. Confirm eGFR.  
- SGLT2 inhibitors: have eGFR thresholds for initiation and have proven cardiorenal benefits for many patients, but require certain eGFR levels and monitoring for genital infections and volume status.  
- GLP‑1 receptor agonists: often safe across a range of eGFRs and provide cardiovascular benefit in many patients with T2D, but can cause GI side effects and interact with depression/weight/food intake issues.  
- Insulin: safest when renal function is reduced (no eGFR restriction) but hypoglycemia risk increases with kidney disease — starts and titration may need close follow‑up.  
- Steroid hyperglycemia: often managed with short‑acting insulin while on prednisone; discuss a plan for temporary insulin if needed.  
- Apixaban: renal impairment affects bleeding risk and may affect apixaban dosing decisions (coordinate with cardiology); also consider that hypoglycemia-related falls/injury while anticoagulated are higher concern.

What to bring to the visit (short checklist)
- Recent labs / ask clinic to run A1c + BMP + UACR + lipid panel before or at visit  
- List of medications with doses and timing (include prednisone details)  
- Home glucose log or meter data, if available  
- A clear statement of symptoms (polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, lightheadedness, falls, hypoglycemia episodes)  
- Ask for a clear plan for steroid periods (how often to check glucose, thresholds for treatment)

Suggested wording if you want me to draft a brief message to Dr. Wilson requesting labs or a referral
- Example: “Hi Dr. Wilson — I’m preparing for my annual and concerned about diabetes risk/management. My last creatinine was 320 (please confirm units/date). Could we run A1c, BMP with eGFR, and urine albumin before the visit? Given my prednisone taper and kidney function, should I be referred to endocrinology (Phoenix Endocrinology) for management/input on medication choices? Thanks — [Your name].”  
If you want, I can draft that as an email or a message to send.

Safety note
