- “Which preventive screens and baseline tests should I have today (eye exam, foot exam, urine albumin, lipid panel, immunizations)?”  
- “Do any of my medications interact with anticoagulation (apixaban) or require coordination (e.g., procedures, dose changes)?”  
- “How often should I follow up and which metrics should I monitor at home (fasting glucose, post-prandial, blood pressure, weight)?”

Labs and checks useful to have before/at the visit
- HbA1c (3 months is ideal)  
- Serum basic metabolic panel: creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes, glucose  
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) for kidney damage screening  
- Lipid panel (total cholesterol, LDL, HDL, triglycerides)  
- Liver function tests if considering certain diabetes meds  
- If not recent: TSH (sometimes useful)  
- Blood pressure, weight/BMI, and any orthostatic vitals if symptomatic  
- Recent fasting glucose logs or continuous glucose monitor data, if used

How your specific conditions/meds affect diabetes management (high-level)
- Prednisone/steroids strongly increase blood glucose while taken — they can unmask or worsen hyperglycemia and sometimes require temporary glucose-lowering treatment or increased monitoring. Bring this up explicitly.  
- Chronic kidney disease (if your creatinine/eGFR confirms impairment) limits or changes the choice/dosing of many diabetes drugs: metformin and SGLT2 inhibitors have renal thresholds to consider, and insulin dosing may need adjustment. Ask your PCP for your current eGFR and whether metformin is safe.  
